{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\nConstruction-time or preview-time repair would violate read-only commands. Retirement completion is safe only after provider preflight and exclusive pass ownership succeed and under-lock staleness validation passes, and it must precede any remote observation so the pass cannot interleave fresh liveness evidence with completion.\n\n## Outcome\nInvoke the S3 recovery operation once at the validated write-bearing reconcile boundary before remote observation; every read-only or filtered path stays zero-write for this operation.\n\n## Verified invocation site (corrected from this task's original impact set)\nThe original plan named `run_differs.py`. That is wrong, and the ordering it asked for is unreachable there. Verified by reading the spine:\n\n- `reconcile._load_snapshots` is called at `reconcile.py:222`; inside it the binding store is constructed (`:280`), the under-lock staleness gate runs (`:281-282`), `scoped_ids` is computed (`:287`), and the FIRST remote issue fetch happens (`:328-333`).\n- `bind_operation_runtime` (the immutable operation/config snapshot) is composed at `:227`, after `_load_snapshots` returns and therefore after that fetch.\n- `run_differs()` is invoked at `:231`, also after the fetch; the existing `recover_pending_bindings` call at `run_differs.py:418` already runs post-fetch.\n\nSo the invocation goes in `reconcile.py`, after the staleness gate and the `scoped_ids` computation and before the fetch. `run_differs.py` is NOT edited by this task. The real size constraint is `reconcile.py` at 794 of the 800-line cap — SIX lines of headroom, and RP-04 S7.3.a is editing the same file — so the spine gets ONE call line plus a short comment, and no new import.\n\n## Plan\n1. Add `BindingRecovery.repair_at_write_boundary(*, persist, scoped)` owning the guard: it returns an empty outcome without any classification or write unless the pass is write-bearing (`persist`) and unscoped (`not scoped`). Expose it through the facade so the spine needs no new import. The unguarded `complete_interrupted_retirements()` from T1 keeps its semantics and stays the direct-test entry point.\n2. Call it from `reconcile.py` on one line, positioned after the under-lock staleness validation and before the first remote issue fetch, binding walk, differ, or lifecycle mutation.\n3. Treat a durable tombstone as authoritative until explicit `unretire`; an automatic 200, `clear_absent`, or same-key confirmation cannot revoke it.\n4. On completion failure, preserve the overlap for retry. Because the call precedes the fetch, a failure structurally cannot have made a remote call.\n\n## Testing\n- Order tests pin the call between the staleness gate and the first fetch, and assert zero provider calls when completion aborts.\n- A read-only table drives construction, load, reconcile-check, dry-run, preview, selection, and filtered paths and asserts zero repository writes attributable to this operation.\n- Guard tests exercise `repair_at_write_boundary` directly across the persist/scoped matrix and prove the non-write-bearing cases do no classification at all.\n- AC5 and AC6 are verified at the PASS level in `test_recovery_write_boundary.py`, not only at the owner level: one test drives a write-bearing pass over a historical overlap whose live record was reconfirmed to the same key and cleared of absence, and asserts the tombstone still completes and survives; a second lifts the tombstone with an explicit `unretire` first and asserts the pass leaves the live binding alone. The owner-level twins already exist from T1 in `state/test_binding_recovery.py` (`test_a_same_key_confirm_does_not_revoke_the_tombstone`, `test_clear_absent_does_not_revoke_the_tombstone`, `test_an_explicit_unretire_removes_the_completion_candidate`); the pass-level pair proves the guarded entry point does not lose that behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] A live write-bearing unscoped pass completes exact recovery after the under-lock staleness gate.\n- [x] That completion happens before the pass's first remote issue observation.\n- [x] Construction, load, reconcile-check, dry-run, preview, selection, and filtered paths perform zero writes from this operation.\n- [x] A completion failure performs zero remote calls and preserves the overlap as recoverable evidence.\n- [x] An automatic success, absence reset, or same-key confirmation does not override the tombstone.\n- [x] Explicit `unretire` removes the tombstone and therefore removes the completion candidate.\n- [x] `run_differs.py` is unmodified and the pre-existing create-recovery gate is unchanged.\n\n## Out of scope\nRich-emission caller cutover, ADR finalization, and any change to the pre-existing create-recovery gate (its no-write-pass remote-write defect is filed separately as `easygoing-tremendous-frogmouth`)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787154049837811001,"uuid":"537d134d-b716-4c0b-ae94-9a8c7bb49cc1"}